 18.\nFor p ≥4, c ≥2,\nN(n, p + 2, {1, 2c}) ⊂N(n, p, {1, 2c}).\nProof.\nN(n, p + 2, {1, 2c}) = N(n −2, p, {1, 2c})\nTheorem 13 twice\n⊂N(n, p, {1, 2c})\nCorollary 11 twice\n□\nTheorem 19.\nLet C1 = {1, 2c1, . . . , 2ck, . . .}, where 2 ≤c1 < · · · < ck < · · · (possibly finite) and let C2 = {1, 2c1}. Then for\nn ≥1,\nGC1(n) = GC2(n).\nProof. We proceed by induction on n. For the base case, it is clear that GC1(1) = GC2(1) = 0. For the induction step, we\nassume the statement is true for all n′ < n. This means that for any p > 1, N(n, p, C1) = N(n, p, C2). Finally, Lemma 18\n∞\n⋃\nimplies that\nN(n, 2x + 1, {1, 2c1}) = N(n, 2c1 + 1, {1, 2c1}), so the last step below holds even if C1 is infinite. Hence\nx=c1\n⎧\n⎨\n⎫\n⎬\nN(n, 2ci + 1, C1)\nGC1(n) = mex\n2ci∈C1\n⎩N(n, 2, C1) ∪\n⋃\n⎭\n⎧\n⎨\n⎫\n⎬\n= mex\nN(n, 2ci + 1, C2)\n2ci∈C1\n⎩N(n, 2, C2) ∪\n⋃\n⎭\n= mex{N(n, 2, C2) ∪N(n, 2c1 + 1, C2)}\nLemma 18\n= GC2(n)\n□\n402--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n36  Tipaluck Krityakierne and Thotsaporn Aek Thanatipanonda (https://thotsaporn.com/Ansatz/ansatz3.pdf)\nciteturn3search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.2 years ago; 36  Tipaluck Krityakierne and Thotsaporn Aek Thanatipanonda ... **Theorem 19. ... **Proof.\n36  Tipaluck Krityakierne and Thotsaporn Aek Thanatipanonda\n\n<visual_element id=\"e1\">\nGray Maple Program box with a clock icon and the label “Maple Program”. Code shown:\n> C2ToDiffHom(N-(c1*a^(n+2)+c2*b^(n+2)),{1,a,b},\n[a0],n,N,x,D);\n</visual_element>\n\nThe next theorem ensures that we can always find a C²-finite recurrence relation for the coefficients aₙ of f(x) which satisfies a homogeneous linear differential equation of composite variables with polynomial coefficients.\n\n**Theorem 19.** Let \\(f(x)=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty} a_n x^n\\). Assume \\(f(x)\\) satisfies a homogeneous linear differential equation with polynomial coefficients of order \\(r\\) and degree \\(k\\)\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\alpha}\\left[q_{\\alpha,0}(x)